<think>
The problem asks us to simulate a process involving $N$ aliens.
We have $N$ aliens, indexed $1$ to $N$.
The $i$-th alien starts with $A_i$ stones.
The $i$-th alien becomes an adult exactly $i$ years later.
When an alien becomes an adult (at year $i$), every *adult* who has at least one stone gives exactly one stone to the newly adult alien.
We need to find the number of stones each alien has after $N$ years.
The constraints are $N \le 5 \times 10^5$ and $A_i \le 5 \times 10^5$.
Since $N$ is up to $5 \times 10^5$, an $O(N^2)$ simulation is too slow. We need something closer to $O(N)$ or $O(N \log N)$.

Let's trace the process.
At year $t$ (where $t$ goes from 1 to $N$), the $t$-th alien becomes an adult.
The condition for giving a stone is: "every adult who has at least one stone".
Who are the adults at year $t$?
The aliens who become adults at year $1, 2, \dots, t$ are adults.
So, at year $t$, aliens $1, 2, \dots, t$ are adults.
Alien $t$ is the one just becoming an adult.
The stones given to alien $t$ come from aliens $1, 2, \dots, t-1$ (since alien $t$ is just becoming adult, they haven't participated in the giving yet, or rather, the problem says "every adult who has at least one stone gives... to the alien who has just become an adult". Usually, this implies existing adults. Let's check the sample explanation).

